WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.140
A robust OpenRouter client starts with a narrow wrapper around the API.

2
00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:16.840
The wrapper should own base URL configuration, authentication, request defaults, timeout policy, retry policy, model allow lists, and error translation.

3
00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:22.380
Application features should call that wrapper instead of constructing raw requests in many places.

4
00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:27.280
This keeps provider and model decisions visible and makes later changes safer.

5
00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:30.360
Timeouts and retries should be explicit.

6
00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:40.860
LLM requests can fail because of network issues, provider capacity, invalid parameters, content policy decisions, malformed input, or context limits.

7
00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:43.440
Retrying every failure is not safe.

8
00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:52.180
Retrying a timeout may be reasonable for an idempotent summarization request, while retrying a request that triggers an external action can duplicate work.

9
00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:58.520
The client should distinguish retryable transport problems from validation errors and policy outcomes.

10
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.120
Inputs and outputs need contracts.

11
00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:13.540
Define maximum input size, expected response format, and how the application handles refusal, truncation, invalid JSON, empty output, or a model answer that does not meet the contract.

12
00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:19.160
If the application expects structured data, validate it with a schema or parser before using it.

13
00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:25.180
If the model output influences business logic, treat it as untrusted until checked.

14
00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:28.580
Configuration should be boring and auditable.

15
00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:34.600
Store allowed models and provider preferences in reviewed configuration, not scattered constants.

16
00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:37.580
Keep secrets out of client-side bundles.

17
00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:40.320
Use generic placeholders in tests.

18
00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:47.540
A clean client layer makes OpenRouter an interchangeable access layer instead of a hidden dependency woven through the product.

19
00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:53.080
That layer also gives incident responders one place to inspect when requests behave unexpectedly.

20
00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:58.700
It also becomes the natural place to enforce rate limits and shared request metadata.

21
00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:02.400
Tests should assert those defaults before teams depend on them.
